n-polling-industry?mode=signed</div>\n</div></div>\n</div>\n<style>\n  .carousel-face { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }\n  .carousel-meta {\n    font-family: var(--mono);\n    font-size: 11px;\n    color: var(--ink-3);\n    letter-spacing: .03em;\n  }\n  .carousel-deck { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; }\n  .carousel-slide {\n    padding: 38px 32px;\n    background: var(--paper, #faf6ef);\n    color: var(--ink, #0c0d10);\n    border: 1px solid #e7e2d6;\n    border-radius: 12px;\n    aspect-ratio: 1080 / 1350;\n    display: flex;\n    flex-direction: column;\n    justify-content: center;\n    align-items: flex-start;\n    position: relative;\n  }\n  .carousel-counter {\n    position: absolute;\n    top: 18px;\n    right: 22px;\n    font-family: var(--mono);\n    font-size: 10.5px;\n    color: var(--ink-3);\n    letter-spacing: .06em;\n  }\n  .carousel-body {\n    font-family: var(--serif);\n    font-size: 26px;\n    line-height: 1.3;\n    font-weight: 600;\n    color: var(--ink);\n  }\n  .carousel-slide-fact .carousel-body { font-weight: 400; font-style: italic; font-size: 22px; }\n  .carousel-slide-closer .carousel-body { font-weight: 400; font-size: 20px; color: var(--ink-3); }\n  .carousel-receipt, .carousel-canonical {\n    position: absolute;\n    bottom: 22px;\n    left: 32px;\n    right: 32px;\n    font-family: var(--mono);\n    font-size: 10.5px;\n    color: var(--ink-3);\n    letter-spacing: .03em;\n  }\n  .carousel-receipt a { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink-3); }\n</style></div>\n</details><details class=\"face\" >\n  <summary>\n    <span class=\"face-label\">Video script</span>\n    <span class=\"face-chip face-ready\">ready</span>\n    <span class=\"face-hint\">Locked reasoning skeleton for a 3–4 minute video — structure, not voice.</span>\n  </summary>\n  <div class=\"face-body\"><p><strong>BEAT 1 — the thing nobody says</strong><br /><ul><li>The Canadian polling industry lacks robust oversight and transparency.</li><li>A dominant market player may compromise industry integrity.</li><li>Weak regulations may enable methodological opacity.</li></ul><br /><strong>BEAT 2 — the instances, dated</strong><br /><ul><li>Canada has weaker disclosure rules compared to the UK's British Polling Council (BPC) [q1].</li><li>The US has the American Association of Public Opinion Research (AAPOR) with robust enforcement mechanisms, unlike Canada [q2].</li><li>338Canada dominates the Canadian polling market as a single aggregator [q3].</li></ul><br /><strong>BEAT 3 — the structure</strong><br /><ul><li>Weak regulatory environment coexists with a dominant market player.</li><li>Lack of independent regulator contributes to methodological opacity.</li><li>Dominant aggregator and weaker rules facilitate a streamlined but less transparent polling environment.</li></ul><br /><strong>BEAT 4 — the implication</strong><br /><ul><li>The industry's integrity and effectiveness may be compromised.</li><li>Further investigation is needed to verify the critical assertion that the industry has been fundamentally compromised.</li></ul></p></div>\n</details><details class=\"face\" >\n  <summary>\n    <span class=\"face-label\">Chapter outline</span>\n    <span class=\"face-chip face-ready\">ready</span>\n    <span class=\"face-hint\">The reading&#39;s structure expanded for long-form treatment.</span>\n  </summary>\n  <div class=\"face-body\"><p>Here is the chapter outline:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Introduction to the Canadian Polling Industry's Regulatory Environment</strong><br /><ul><li>The Canadian polling industry has weaker disclosure rules compared to other countries, such as the UK's British Polling Council (BPC)</li><li>Canada lacks an independent regulator with robust enforcement mechanisms, unlike the US's American Association of Public Opinion Research (AAPOR) [needs deeper sourcing]</li></ul><br /><strong>2. Dominance of a Single Market Player</strong><br /><ul><li>The Canadian polling industry is dominated by a single aggregator, 338Canada</li><li>This dominance may contribute to a more streamlined but less transparent polling environment</li></ul><br /><strong>3. Criticisms of the Industry's State</strong><br /><ul><li>Critics argue that the industry has been 'hollowed out into a federally subsidized, methodologically opaque, AI-experimentation playground'</li><li>This critical view suggests that the industry's issues run deep and are fundamentally compromising its integrity</li></ul><br /><strong>4. Comparison to Other Countries' Regulatory Environments</strong><br /><ul><li>The UK's BPC has robust disclosure rules and enforcement mechanisms</li><li>The US's AAPOR also has robust enforcement mechanisms, which Canada lacks [needs deeper sourcing]</li></ul><br /><strong>5. Potential Implications of Weak Oversight and Dominant Market Player</strong><br /><ul><li>The combination of weak oversight and a dominant market player may facilitate a less transparent polling environment</li><li>The impact of these factors on the industry's overall integrity and effectiveness remains a topic for further investigation</li></ul><br /><strong>6. The Need for Further Investigation</strong><br /><ul><li>The critical assertion that the industry has been fundamentally compromised remains a viewpoint, not a verified fact</li><li>Further research is needed to verify the extent of the industry's issues and their impact on its integrity and effectiveness</li></ul><br /><strong>7. Potential Research Directions</strong><br /><ul><li>Investigating the specific effects of weak oversight and dominant market player on polling accuracy and transparency</li><li>Comparing the Canadian polling industry to other countries with stronger regulatory environments</li></ul><br /><strong>8. Conclusion</strong><br /><ul><li>The Canadian polling industry operates with relatively weak oversight and a dominant market player, which may have significant implications for its integrity and effectiveness</li><li>Further research is needed to fully understand the impact of these factors and to inform potential reforms.</li></ul></p></div>\n</details>\n</section>\n<style>\n  .faces { margin:36px 0 8px; padding:18px 20px; border:1px solid var(--rule);\n    border-radius:11px; background:var(--panel-soft, rgba(0,0,0,.02)); }\n  .faces-head { margin-bottom:12px; }\n  .faces-k { font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.14em; text-transform:uppercase;\n    color:var(--ink-3); font-weight:600; margin-bottom:4px; }\n  .faces-sub { font-size:12.5px; color:var(--ink-3); font-style:italic; }\n  .face { border-top:1px solid var(--rule); padding:11px 0; }\n  .face:first-of-type { border-top:none; }\n  .face summary { cursor:pointer; display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px;\n    flex-wrap:wrap; list-style:none; user-select:none; }\n  .face summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }\n  .face-label { font-family:var(--serif); font-size:16px; color:var(--ink); }\n  .face-chip { font-size:9.5px; letter-spacing:.08em; padding:2px 7px; border-radius:3px;\n    text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:600; }\n  .face-ready { background:var(--confirmed-bg, #e7f0e6); color:var(--confirmed, #2f7d52); }\n  .face-fail-chip { background:var(--correction-bg, #f6e4df); color:var(--correction, #b0402c); }\n  .face-pending-chip { background:rgba(0,0,0,.06); color:var(--ink-3); }\n  .face-hint { font-size:12px; color:var(--ink-3); margin-left:auto; flex:1; text-align:right; min-width:200px; }\n  .face-body { margin-top:12px; font-size:14.5px; line-height:1.65; color:var(--ink-2); }\n  .face-body p { margin-bottom:10px; }\n  .face-body strong { color:var(--ink); }\n  .face-fail { margin-top:10px; padding:9px 12px; border-radius:6px;\n    background:var(--correction-bg, #f6e4df); color:var(--correction, #b0402c); font-size:13px; }\n  .face-pending { margin-top:10px; padding:9px 12px; border-radius:6px;\n    background:rgba(0,0,0,.04); color:var(--ink-3); font-size:13p